 "customerTurns": 5,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Hi, our integrated fridge freezer (behind a kitchen door) - the fridge is around 10 degrees but the freezer seems ok. We had a power cut a week ago, not sure if related. Rented flat, can't really take it out."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the fan or air channel that moves cold air into the fridge. Is the freezer still cold while the fridge is warm, and is there a thick block of ice on the back wall inside the freezer?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Yes, the freezer is still cold (about -16C) and the fridge is about 10C. There's some frost on the back wall of the freezer but not a thick block, just a bit."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the automatic defrost system. Behind the rear panel inside the freezer, are the coils a solid block of ice, or fairly clear?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can't get at the back panel of the freezer, I'm only seeing a bit of frost on the back wall, not a solid block. I also can't take anything apart as it's a rented flat and it's screwed into the cabinet."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the fan or air channel that moves cold air into the fridge. Cold air comes in through vents, often on the back wall inside. Make sure food isn't pressed against them and the shelves aren't packed so tight air can't move. Were the vents blocked or was it over-packed (and have you moved things), or was everything clear?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can't actually see any vent in the fridge part, so I don't know if anything is blocking one. I'm not sure about how packed it is either. There's no burning smell and nothing has tripped, if that helps. Is there anything else I can check without taking it out?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you can check if the vents at the back of the fridge compartment are blocked by food items or ice. You should also ensure the appliance is level and that the door seals are closing properly to maintain cold air circulation. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the automatic defrost system. Run your fingers round the rubber seal on the door and look for splits, gaps or a section that has come away, especially where the frost is. Is the seal torn or come away, was it dirty or folded (and is that sorted), or does it look fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The fridge door closes fine and the cabinet door moves with it. I can't see any splits or bits coming away on the seal, it looks ok to me."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
